  "text": "**Solution 3 is the only one with the right answer, 4.** I checked the key cases by hand.\n\n**Isolation number of paths.** A closed neighborhood covers at most 3 consecutive path vertices, and uncovered vertices must be pairwise non-adjacent. With k vertices in S, at most 3k + (k+1) vertices can be handled, so ι(P_n) = ⌈(n−1)/4⌉. This gives ι(P_6) = ι(P_7) = ι(P_8) = ι(P_9) = 2 and ι(P_10) = 3.\n\n**Lower bound from P_6.** Subdividing k edges of P_6 gives P_{6+k}, since every subdivision of a path is a path. For k ≤ 3, the result is P_6 through P_9, all with ι = 2. For k = 4, the result is P_10, with ι = 3. So sd_ι(P_6) = 4, and the maximum is at least 4.\n\n**Solution 1 (answer 5)** is wrong. It claims ι(P_10) = 2, but two closed neighborhoods cover at most 6 vertices, leaving at least 4 uncovered vertices in at most 3 gaps, so some gap contains an edge and ι(P_10) = 3. This makes sd_ι(P_6) = 4, not 5.\n\n**Solution 2 (answer 2)** is wrong. It checks only P_4 and then extrapolates to a general bound of 2, which P_6 already refutes.\n\n**Solution 3 (answer 4)** has the right value and correct reasoning for P_6. Its general upper bound for all non-star trees is argued only loosely, so I am relying on my recollection of the literature for the claim that no non-star tree exceeds 4, not on a full verification.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
